🌡️ Automatic Fan Control using STM32 (DHT11)
📌 Overview

This project implements an Automatic Fan Control System using an STM32 microcontroller and a DHT11 temperature sensor. The system continuously monitors ambient temperature and automatically switches a fan ON or OFF based on predefined threshold values.

🎯 Features
Real-time temperature monitoring
Digital sensor interfacing (DHT11)
Automatic fan control
Simple embedded control logic
Energy-efficient operation
🧠 Working Principle

The DHT11 sensor measures ambient temperature and sends digital data to the STM32. The microcontroller processes this data and compares it with a threshold value. If the temperature exceeds the set limit, the fan is turned ON; otherwise, it remains OFF.

🔧 Hardware Used
STM32 (e.g., STM32F4 series / Trainer Kit)
DHT11 Temperature Sensor
Fan / LED (as output indicator)
Connecting wires
🔌 Pin Configuration
Component	Pin
DHT11 Data Pin	PA9
Fan Control	PA10
⚙️ Software Configuration
IDE: STM32CubeIDE
Language: C (HAL Drivers)
Timing: DWT-based microsecond delay
Communication: Single-wire digital protocol
📊 Output
If temperature > 30°C → Fan ON
If temperature ≤ 30°C → Fan OFF
🚀 How It Works
STM32 sends a start signal to DHT11
Sensor responds with temperature data
Data is read bit-by-bit using precise timing
Temperature value is extracted
Fan is controlled based on threshold
System updates every 2 seconds
▶️ How to Run
Open project in STM32CubeIDE
Build the project
Connect STM32 via ST-Link/USB
Flash the code
Observe fan operation based on temperature
⚠️ Notes
Use a 4.7k–10kΩ pull-up resistor on DHT11 data pin
Ensure DWT timer is enabled for accurate delays
Avoid long wires to prevent unstable readings
Valid temperature range check is included (30–60°C)
📁 Project Structure
